/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  Component: d,l-Malic acid (hydroxybutanedioic acid), crystal (C4H6O5).  MW 134.087 g/mol (the engine's atomic weights).

  A NON-VOLATILE crystalline organic solid (mp 128-129 C, Perry's 9th ed. Table 2-2, p. 2-38): never enters the vapour (K = 0),
  no Antoine block.  Built on the citric-acid pattern (components/citricAcid.dat); the C372 campaign had left
  this acid without a component, and C373 builds it from the formation data of Miller & Smith-Magowan (1990),
  with Domalski (1972) as the second reading.

  WHAT THIS RECORD CARRIES, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT.

    carries   the crystal formation enthalpy dHf = -1105.66 +/- 0.63 kJ/mol
              (Miller & Smith-Magowan, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 19 (1990) 1049, Table 5, p. 1056, row DL-Malic, dfH (their ref. b: Cox & Pilcher 1970; read from a page image));
              the crystal density 1.601 g/cm3 at 20/4 C (Perry's);
              the heat of solution of the pair file parameters/solution/malicAcid-water.dat.
    does NOT  a standard entropy s_298 and any heat capacity: no work in hand prints them for this crystal
              as a record value (Miller prints S of the crystal only for succinic and fumaric acid, and it
              enters the ions' entropies through the third-law route of its Table 2, not this record).  An
              energy balance that reaches the crystal's or the solution's SENSIBLE heat therefore refuses by
              name, which is the honest behaviour (CLAUDE.md rule 5).

    ALSO BUILT, in their own homes: the malate family (H2Malic, HMalic, Malic; chemistry/*-formation); pK 3.459 / 5.097 (Eden & Bates 1959, Table 6, p. 169).
              Dissolved, this crystal is the molecular master of the family (the hypothetical non-dissociated
              acid); a case that needs the dissociation declares that master.
              Second reading: Domalski, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 1 (1972) 221, Section 6.14, p. 236: DL-malic acid (c) dHf -264.27 kcal/mol x 4.184 = -1105.7 kJ/mol (Section 5.5, p. 225), a second reading in agreement; the L acid is -263.78 kcal/mol; read from a page image.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

name        malicAcid;
formula     C4H6O5;
MW          134.087;            // kg/kmol

role        nonvolatile;               // crystalline organic acid; never to the vapour (K = 0)
dissociation 1;                 // molecular (the ions are species/, see the header)

solid
{
    rho_p   1601.0;             // crystal density [kg/m^3] [1.601 g/cm3 at 20/4 C, Perry's 9th ed., Table 2-2]
}

standardThermochemistry
{
    dHf_298   -1105660;        // J/mol  crystalline [-1105.66 +/- 0.63 kJ/mol, Miller & Smith-Magowan 1990]
    referenceState     pureSolid;
}

provenance
{
    dHf_298
    {
        origin        literature;
        method        "evaluated enthalpy of formation of the crystal (-1105.66 +/- 0.63 kJ/mol); second reading: Domalski, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 1 (1972) 221, Section 6.14, p. 236: DL-malic acid (c) dHf -264.27 kcal/mol x 4.184 = -1105.7 kJ/mol (Section 5.5, p. 225), a second reading in agreement; the L acid is -263.78 kcal/mol; read from a page image";
        citation      "Miller & Smith-Magowan, J. Phys. Chem. Ref. Data 19 (1990) 1049, Table 5, p. 1056, row DL-Malic, dfH (their ref. b: Cox & Pilcher 1970; read from a page image)";
    }
    rho_p
    {
        origin        literature;
        method        "tabulated crystal density";
        citation      "Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook, 9th ed., Table 2-2, p. 2-38, row Malic acid (dl-) (1.601 g/cm3 at 20/4 C)";
    }
}
